## Summary of Modifications

The patch fixes an out-of-bounds array access vulnerability in multiple AMD Display Core (DCN) resource files. The bug occurs in stream encoder creation functions where the `eng_id` parameter (of type `enum engine_id`, which is signed) is used directly as an index into the `stream_enc_regs[]` array. The original code checked `eng_id <= ENGINE_ID_DIGF` (value 5), but the array has only 5 elements (indices 0–4), so index 5 is out of bounds. Additionally, negative `eng_id` values were not rejected.

### Modifications in Each File

The same change is applied to six files:

- `drivers/gpu/drm/amd/display/dc/resource/dcn35/dcn35_resource.c`
- `drivers/gpu/drm/amd/display/dc/resource/dcn351/dcn351_resource.c`
- `drivers/gpu/drm/amd/display/dc/resource/dcn315/dcn315_resource.c`
- `drivers/gpu/drm/amd/display/dc/resource/dcn316/dcn316_resource.c`
- `drivers/gpu/drm/amd/display/dc/resource/dcn321/dcn321_resource.c`
- `drivers/gpu/drm/amd/display/dc/resource/dcn32/dcn32_resource.c`

In each file, the `stream_encoder_create` function originally contained:

```c
if (eng_id <= ENGINE_ID_DIGF) {
    vpg_inst = eng_id;
    afmt_inst = eng_id;
} else
    return NULL;
```

This is replaced with:

```c
if (eng_id < 0 || eng_id >= ARRAY_SIZE(stream_enc_regs))
    return NULL;

vpg_inst = eng_id;
afmt_inst = eng_id;
```

The new condition uses `ARRAY_SIZE()` to enforce a proper upper bound and explicitly rejects negative `eng_id` values. The assignments to `vpg_inst` and `afmt_inst` are now unconditional, as the guard ensures they are only reached with valid indices.

### Relationships Between Changes

All six modifications address the same underlying bug—an out-of-bounds index into the `stream_enc_regs[]` array—in closely related DCN hardware variants. The fix is identical across all files because the vulnerable code pattern was duplicated when adding support for different DCN generations. By centralizing the bounds check with `ARRAY_SIZE()`, the patch ensures consistent prevention of array overrun in all affected stream encoder creation functions.

The code statements that lead to the bug are:
if (eng_id <= ENGINE_ID_DIGF) {
    vpg_inst = eng_id;
    afmt_inst = eng_id;
} else
    return NULL;
...
&stream_enc_regs[eng_id],
Reason: The check `eng_id <= ENGINE_ID_DIGF` allows `eng_id = 5` (ENGINE_ID_DIGF) which exceeds the valid index range 0–4 for the `stream_enc_regs[5]` array. Negative values also pass this check, leading to out-of-bounds access when indexing the array.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (eng_id < 0 || eng_id >= ARRAY_SIZE(stream_enc_regs))
    return NULL;
vpg_inst = eng_id;
afmt_inst = eng_id;
...
&stream_enc_regs[eng_id],
Reason: The new check rejects any `eng_id` that is negative or greater than or equal to the array size (5), ensuring only valid indices 0–4 are used. The assignments are placed after the check, preserving correct mapping without an else branch.

